{%- doc -%}
  Category eyebrow for a product card (e.g. "SAUCES & CHUTNEYS").

  CURRENT SCOPE (2026-08-25, third revision — see ARCHITECTURE.md §25/§31/§32
  for the full history, this comment states only the end state): wired into
  templates/collection.occasion.json (occasion pages) and templates/
  index.json (Top Sellers rail) only. Deliberately absent from templates/
  collection.all.json (Shop All) and templates/collection.json (Tea/
  Condiments/Pantry) — briefly added there 2026-08-25, removed again
  2026-08-25 once the client clarified the design only wants it on occasion
  pages and the Top Sellers rail, not the plain category grids.

  This is a per-template distinction, not a per-card-type one: both Card A
  (bordered, occasion pages) and Card B (borderless, Top Sellers) render
  through the exact same `_product-card`/`_product-card-gallery`/
  `product-title`/`price` block family as Shop All and the three category
  pages — the only thing that differs is which templates' `block_order`
  include this block. Nothing reads collection metadata, tags, or any other
  signal to decide — a newly created collection shows the eyebrow or doesn't
  purely by which template it's assigned (occasion vs. default), the same
  mechanism that already separates the two card treatments. There is
  deliberately no second card component and no runtime toggle to keep in
  sync.

  Not a generic `text` block bound to a dynamic source: `{{ closest.product.type
  }}` was tried earlier this project and rejected outright by Shopify's own
  template validator (a real sync error, not assumed) — dynamic sources on a
  text block's setting are checked against a fixed allow-list, and product
  type/vendor aren't on it. This block reads `closest.product` directly in
  compiled Liquid instead, which isn't subject to that same editor-bound
  validation.

  Also not actually built on `product.type` even though that's the
  conceptually obvious field — confirmed live via /products.json that
  `product_type` is empty on every product on this store. `vendor` is just
  "Lambruk Pantry" on every product too. `tags` is the one field that
  reliably carries a real category (`tea` / `condiments` / `pantry`) — it's
  already the whole basis of blocks/lambruk-tag-filter.liquid, so this block
  reuses that same tag set and the same case-insensitive matching, and the
  same display labels, so the two stay consistent. A product with none of
  the three known tags renders nothing rather than guessing.

  The design's example copy ("SAUCES & CHUTNEYS · 260G") also includes a
  size suffix. Left out on purpose: sizes aren't a separate structured
  field on this store's products (all variants are "Default Title"; the
  size lives in the product's own title text, e.g. "Iced Tea 60g Loose
  Leaf") — extracting it would mean regex-guessing against free-text
  titles with inconsistent formats, not reading real data.
{%- enddoc -%}

{% liquid
  assign label = nil
  for tag in closest.product.tags
    assign downcased_tag = tag | downcase
    case downcased_tag
      when 'tea'
        assign label = 'Tea'
      when 'condiments'
        assign label = 'Sauces & Chutneys'
      when 'pantry'
        assign label = 'Pantry & Gifts'
    endcase
  endfor
%}

{% if label %}
  <p
    class="product-category-label spacing-style"
    style="{% render 'spacing-style', settings: block.settings %}"
    {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
  >
    {{ label }}
  </p>
{% endif %}

{% stylesheet %}
  .product-category-label {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body--family);
    font-size: var(--text-3-size);
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #A07037;
  }
{% endstylesheet %}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:names.product_category_label",
  "tag": null,
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "t:content.padding"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding-block-start",
      "label": "t:settings.top",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 40,
      "step": 1,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding-block-end",
      "label": "t:settings.bottom",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 40,
      "step": 1,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 4
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding-inline-start",
      "label": "t:settings.left",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 40,
      "step": 1,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding-inline-end",
      "label": "t:settings.right",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 40,
      "step": 1,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 0
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
